Change(genesis='e30427e699992327267add669c3bc989f6d16525ba6f493121eec7778638e0ce', deps=('1ca0212bda3f13eb32308f92eb1109a7949c8b62f36052589e4d91c928dcba92',), event=Authored(todo='gateverdicts', at=datetime(2026, 11, 21, 21, 3, 42, 388754), actor='bmabsout', kind='todo', created=datetime(2026, 11, 21, 0, 0, 0), body="A train's verdict says what it is a verdict of: the live half's is of the tree and the data it read, and a red the operator has traced to a cause outside the tree can be set aside on the record", spec=Flat(value=0.35), rationale=('On 2026-11-21 three sound changes were stopped: one by a state of the', 'live store, two by a flaky law and a failed cache job. Each then needed', 'a new commit, a new review of it and a whole new train, because a tree', 'that was red once cannot land.'), category='', waiting_on='', detail="Two changes, both small and both in the verdict table. One: the live half reads the owner's data, so its row is keyed by the tree and by the heads of the stores it read; a change of the data is then a new question, not a second answer to the old one, and no finding. Say also which of the live half's steps hold a merge and which belong before a deploy and in the nightly run, where the pre-deploy comparison already is: a step that fails on a state of the data is a fault of the scene, found at any time, and should not stop a change that touches neither the web nor the server. Two: a red row can be set aside by the operator at a terminal with a reason that names an open task (a flake, a failed job of the runners), which the table keeps and the land prints; the gate then runs again for the same tree. This replaces making a new tree by a commit that changes nothing, which hid the same fact at the cost of a train. What stays: a red nobody has explained stops the land, and a tree with both verdicts and no reason is still a finding.\n\nA fourth kind, seen the same day: a guard of the live half reads commit messages (a declared growth), so a sentence in a message turned a train red, and a reworded message changes no tree, so the red row would have stood for a tree that was never at fault. A verdict keyed by the tree may read nothing but the tree: say for each step of the gate what it reads beside the tree (the store's data, the messages since the merge base, the runners' state), and key or exempt it by that.", source=None, subtodos=(), notes=(), note=''))